course: dictionary
lesson: 9476
title: Statues in our world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A statue is a model of a person or an animal, made of stone, metal or wood. It is usually life-size or bigger, and it often stands in a public place, like a square or a park. Context clues help you spot this: «The statue stood in the square, watching over the town.»
explain: Read the words around statue to check your idea. Stood, square and watching all show a big, still figure. Now swap in your meaning: "The model of a person stood in the square, watching over the town" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Statues can also remember people. Towns put up statues of important people so that everyone who walks past remembers them. Some statues are famous all over the world, like the Statue of Liberty in New York.
fact: A statue is a model of a person or animal, made of stone, metal or wood.
fact: A statue is usually life-size or bigger, and it is often in a public place.
fact: «The Great Sphinx» is a huge statue with a lion's body and a pharaoh's head, carved from rock.
fact: Statues can remember important people, and the Statue of Liberty is a giant statue holding up a torch.
fact: In musical statues, players freeze very still, like a statue, when the music stops.
exercise: «The statue stood in the square, watching over the town.» What is a statue? | A kind of bird that sings in the square | *A model of a person or animal, made of stone, metal or wood | A tall, thin tree that grows in towns | A bright light for the night
exercise: «The Great Sphinx is a huge statue with a lion's body and a pharaoh's head, carved from the rock next to the pyramids.» What was the Great Sphinx carved from? | Wood | *Rock | Cloth | Glass
exercise: Find and copy one word from «a giant statue holding up a torch» that means very large. | holding | statue | torch | *giant
test: Ella says a statue is always made of metal. Is she right? | Yes, a statue is always made of metal | Yes, but only if it is small | *No, a statue can be made of stone, metal or wood | No, statues are always made of paper
test: In the playground game «musical statues», what do players do when the music stops? | Sit down on the grass and chat | Run to the swings and climb the frame | *Freeze very still, like a statue | Dance faster until the teacher blows a whistle
exam: «At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines that built bronze statues of Zeus with their names carved on the base.» Why did everyone walking into the stadium remember the cheats? | They won gold medals for their town | *Their names were carved on the bases of the statues | They were the judges of the races | They were given a statue of their own to keep
